A stack and a queue are two fundamental data structures used in computer science. They both have a similar structure, but they operate differently.

A stack is a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) data structure. This means that the last item that is added to the stack is the first item to be removed. The main operation of a stack is the push() operation, which adds an item to the top of the stack, and the pop() operation, which removes the item from the top of the stack.

A queue is a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) data structure. This means that the first item that is added to the queue is the first item to be removed. The main operation of a queue is the enqueue() operation, which adds an item to the end of the queue, and the dequeue() operation, which removes the item from the front of the queue.

Here is an example of pseudocode for a stack:
```
class Stack {
    private int[] items;
    private int top;

    public Stack(int capacity) {
        items = new int[capacity];
        top = -1;
    }

    public void push(int item) {
        if (top == capacity) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack overflow");
        }
        items[++top] = item;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack underflow");
        }
        int item = items[top--];
        items[top] = null; // to avoid null pointer exception
        return item;
    }
}
```
And here is an example of Java code for a queue:
```
class Queue {
    private int[] items;
    private int front;
    private int rear;

    public Queue(int capacity) {
        items = new int[capacity];
        front = -1;
        rear = -1;
    }

    public void enqueue(int item) {
        if (front == rear) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Queue overflow");
        }
        rear++;
        items[rear] = item;
    }

    public int dequeue() {
        if (front == rear) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Queue underflow");
        }
        int item = items[front];
        front++;
        items[front] = null; // to avoid null pointer exception
        return item;
    }
}
```
